```markdown
---
title: "Centralized Logging Platform"
category: "Observability & Reliability"
difficulty: "Hard"
tags: ["logging", "observability", "pii", "redaction", "search", "kafka", "opensearch", "s3", "clickhouse"]
---

## Overview

This system centralizes application and infrastructure logs with three guarantees: (1) PII is redacted before it becomes searchable, (2) schema evolution never breaks ingestion, and (3) engineers can do fast, time-bounded text search with predictable latency.

The key insight is to **separate “what is safe and fast to search” from “what is cheap and complete to retain.”** We keep a **strict, stable log envelope** (timestamp/service/level/trace/span/message/attrs) and treat everything else as schemaless attributes, while enforcing an **ingestion-time redaction boundary** that nothing crosses uninspected. Hot, fast search lives in OpenSearch; long retention and reprocessing live in object storage in Parquet.

## What Makes This Hard

Naive logging platforms fail in two places:
1) **PII leaks via “just index everything.”** Once unredacted content lands in a search index, it gets replicated, cached, snapshotted, and copied into too many places to claw back.
2) **Schema drift turns into operational drift.** Dynamic mappings explode (OpenSearch) or schemas ossify (SQL), and teams either lose fields silently or break ingestion during deploys.

The trap is treating logs as “just text.” Logs are **semi-structured events** with privacy constraints and lifecycle management; you need a redaction boundary, a schema strategy, and a retention strategy that reinforce each other.

## Requirements

### Functional Requirements
- Ingest logs from services/hosts with at-least-once delivery and backpressure.
- Support schema evolution without coordinated deploys across producers and consumers.
- Enforce PII redaction/masking before any data becomes broadly accessible/searchable.
- Provide fast text search over recent logs with filters (time range, service, level, trace_id) and “tail -f” experience.
- Retain logs cost-effectively for compliance/forensics and allow reprocessing when parsers/redaction rules change.

### Scale Targets
- **Ingest:** 250k events/s average, 1M events/s peak (incident storms); avg 1.5KB/event → ~375MB/s avg, ~1.5GB/s peak.
- **Hot search retention:** 7 days indexed (engineers debug the “now”); **p95 query < 2s** for last 15 minutes, < 5s for last 24h.
- **Cold retention:** 180 days in object storage (cost + investigations); partitioned for batch scans.
- **Freshness:** searchable within 10 seconds (incident response requires near-real-time, not streaming-perfect).

## Key Design Decisions

- **Redaction boundary at ingestion**
  - Chose: a mandatory stream processing stage that parses + classifies + redacts before indexing.
  - Rejected: “agents redact” as the only control (too easy to bypass/misconfigure), and “redact at query time” (too late; index already contains PII).
  - Why: privacy failures are irreversible once indexed; centralized enforcement is auditable and consistent.

- **Stable envelope + schemaless attributes**
  - Chose: fixed top-level columns + `attrs` map for evolving fields; strict controls against mapping explosion.
  - Rejected: fully dynamic indexing of arbitrary JSON (mapping explosion) and fully rigid schemas (constant migrations, dropped fields).
  - Why: envelope enables fast filters and joins (trace_id/service/time); attrs preserves agility.

- **Two-tier storage: OpenSearch (hot) + S3/Parquet (cold)**
  - Chose: OpenSearch for low-latency text search; S3/Parquet for cheap retention and reprocessing.
  - Rejected: OpenSearch-only (expensive, operationally brittle at long retention) and S3-only (slow interactive search).
  - Why: different workloads want different storage; pretending one engine does both well creates on-call pain.

## Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A[Agents] --> B[Ingest Gateway]
  B --> C[Kafka]
  C --> D[Redact + Enrich]
  D --> E[OpenSearch (7d)]
  D --> F[S3 Parquet (180d)]
  G[Query API] --> E
  G --> F
  H[UI + CLI] --> G
```

### Components

- **Agents (Fluent Bit/Vector):** ship logs with local buffering and backpressure; add host metadata; never trusted for redaction.
- **Ingest Gateway:** authn/authz, tenant routing, rate limits, gzip, request validation; returns explicit backpressure signals.
- **Kafka:** the shock absorber; isolates producers from downstream outages and enables reprocessing from a known offset.
- **Redact + Enrich (Flink/Kafka Streams):** the enforcement point: parse structured logs, normalize into envelope, apply PII policy, add derived fields (service, env, trace_id), and route to sinks.
- **OpenSearch (hot):** fast time-bounded text search + filters; index lifecycle management (ILM) rolls indices daily/hourly.
- **S3 Parquet (cold):** immutable, partitioned by `dt/hour/service`; supports cheap retention and batch queries (Trino/Athena/Spark).
- **Query API:** one interface for search/tail/export with consistent auth, field projections, and audit logs.
- **UI + CLI:** “tail”, saved queries, incident views, and trace-log pivoting.

## Deep Dive: PII Redaction With Guarantees (Without Killing Search)

The hardest part is making “PII-safe” a property of the pipeline, not a best-effort convention. The design uses three layers that reinforce each other:

1) **Allowlist-first schema handling.** The only fields promoted to top-level indexed columns are an explicit allowlist (`timestamp`, `service`, `level`, `trace_id`, `span_id`, `message`, `attrs`). Everything else stays inside `attrs` as strings. This prevents accidental indexing of new fields that appear during deploys.

2) **Deterministic masking for search utility.** For common PII types (email, phone, IP, account ids), the processor replaces matches with typed tokens:
   - `alice@example.com` → `<email:sha256=…>`
   - `+1-415-…` → `<phone:sha256=…>`
   This preserves the ability to answer “did we log this customer’s email?” via hashing, without revealing it broadly. If you need reversibility, you do it explicitly with a vault-backed tokenization service and tight access controls; irreversible hashing is the default because it’s operationally safe.

3) **Post-redaction verification + quarantine.** After masking, the processor runs a second detection pass. If it still detects high-confidence PII (or parsing fails), it routes the event to a **quarantine topic** that is:
   - not indexed,
   - short-retention,
   - restricted access,
   - fully audited.
This prevents silent leakage and gives a clean operational workflow: fix rule → replay Kafka offsets → backfill safe outputs.

This approach teaches a non-obvious lesson: **the only reliable way to prevent PII in search is to treat indexing as a privilege granted after verification**, not as the default outcome of ingestion.

## Trade-offs

| Optimized For | Sacrificed |
|--------------|------------|
| PII safety by construction | Raw-log convenience in search |
| Predictable search latency (hot window) | Single-store simplicity |
| Schema agility without breakage | Some fields stay unindexed unless promoted |
| Reprocessing and auditability | Extra pipeline stage to operate |

## Failure Modes

- **OpenSearch cluster degradation (red/yellow, high heap, slow queries)**
  - Happens: indexing lags; queries time out.
  - Detect: ingestion lag to OpenSearch, rejected writes, heap pressure, query p95 spikes.
  - Recover: throttle/route via Kafka, scale hot nodes, shrink index mappings, roll over indices more frequently, temporarily reduce indexed fields (keep envelope + message).

- **PII redaction regression**
  - Happens: new pattern leaks PII; risk is systemic.
  - Detect: post-redaction verifier hits, anomaly alerts on token ratios, periodic DLP scans of indexed samples.
  - Recover: quarantine + stop-index switch (feature flag), replay from Kafka after rule fix, rotate compromised snapshots if required by policy.

- **Kafka backlog during incident storm**
  - Happens: downstream can’t keep up; lag grows; “near real-time” becomes minutes.
  - Detect: consumer lag alarms per partition, ingest gateway backpressure events.
  - Recover: scale processors horizontally, reduce enrichment cost, shed non-critical tenants, and keep acceptance high by prioritizing the redaction stage over indexing.

## What I'd Do Differently At...

- **10x scale:** Move to tiered Kafka storage, shard by tenant/service, add dedicated “tail” path (WebSocket) backed by a small hot cache of recent events, and enforce per-tenant budgets to stop one team from melting search.
- **100x scale:** OpenSearch becomes the bottleneck/cost center; re-architect hot search into (a) smaller “recent” window in OpenSearch and (b) high-performance columnar search (ClickHouse with text indexes) for broader interactive ranges, with strict query governance and pre-aggregated incident views.

## Operational Notes

- Keep OpenSearch mappings tight: index only a small set of fields; store `attrs` as a single flattened object or non-indexed blob to prevent mapping explosion.
- Enforce index lifecycle: roll daily/hourly, hot→warm→delete; size shards for recovery speed, not just throughput.
- Treat redaction rules as code: versioned, reviewed, tested against fixtures; deploy with canaries and a “stop indexing” kill switch.
- Build “replay by offset” tooling: every incident response eventually needs “reprocess yesterday with the new parser.”
- Audit everything that touches quarantine or any reversible tokenization; assume those logs will be requested during an investigation.
